  "raw_output": "{\n  \"exercise_type\": \"CEFR English Placement Test\",\n  \"section_id\": 5,\n  \"section_title\": \"Reading Comprehension\",\n  \"cefr_range\": \"B2-C1\",\n  \"reading_text\": \"The concept of sustainability has evolved significantly over the past few decades. Initially, it was associated with environmental protection and conservation efforts. However, as our understanding of the interconnectedness of economic, social, and environmental systems deepened, the definition expanded to encompass not only ecological but also economic and social aspects.\n\nToday, sustainable development is seen as a holistic approach that balances human needs with environmental preservation. This shift in perspective has led to the development of new technologies, policies, and practices aimed at reducing waste, promoting renewable energy sources, and fostering equitable economic growth.\n\nDespite these advancements, many challenges remain. The pace of technological progress often outstrips our ability to adapt and regulate its impact on society. Furthermore, the pursuit of short-term gains can compromise long-term sustainability goals, highlighting the need for a more nuanced understanding of this complex concept.\n\nAs we move forward, it is essential to recognize that sustainability is not merely an environmental issue but also a human rights concern. It requires a fundamental shift in our values and priorities, one that prioritizes the well-being of both people and planet.\",\n  \"questions\": [\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 36,\n      \"question\": \"What was the initial focus of the concept of sustainability?\",\n      \"options\": [\"Environmental protection only\", \"Economic growth primarily\", \"Social justice initially\", \"Environmental protection and conservation efforts\"],\n      \"answer\": \"4\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 37,\n      \"question\": \"According to the text, what is the main difference between old and new approaches to sustainability?\",\n      \"options\": [\"Old: focused on environment; New: focused on economy\", \"Old: focused on people; New: focused on planet\", \"Old: narrow definition; New: holistic approach\", \"Old: short-term goals; New: long-term focus\"],\n      \"answer\": \"3\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 38,\n      \"question\": \"What is the author's attitude towards the pace of technological progress?\",\n      \"options\": [\"It is too slow\", \"It is just right\", \"It often outstrips our ability to adapt and regulate its impact\", \"It has no negative consequences\"],\n      \"answer\": \"3\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 39,\n      \"question\": \"What does the text imply about the relationship between economic growth and sustainability?\",\n      \"options\": [\"They are mutually exclusive\", \"One is necessary for the other\", \"They can coexist but with caution\", \"Short-term gains often compromise long-term goals\"],\n      \"answer\": \"4\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"question_number\": 40,\n      \"question\": \"What does the author consider essential for moving forward in terms of sustainability?\",\n      \"options\": [\"More investment in renewable energy sources\", \"A fundamental shift in our values and priorities\", \"Increased focus on environmental protection only\", \"Greater emphasis on economic growth\"],\n      \"answer\": \"2\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
